\b\f1\fs18\cf1 How to configure and save your wireless adapter settings
\par }\pard \ql \li180\ri0\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 {\f1\fs18\cf1 The settings you make on this page do not take effect until you click Apply. The status bar at the b
ottom of this page displays real time details about your wireless connection. Once you apply the settings, they will remain until you change them. You can also create and save multiple profiles and activate whichever one you need to use.
\par }\pard \ql \li180\ri0\sb60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 {\b\f1\fs18\cf1 How to Configure Settings
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 \f1\fs18\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
1. Choose the Network Name SSID of your network. The drop-down list will show all the SSID names being broadcast at your location. The corresponding Infrastructure or Ad-Hoc setting is automatically made.
\par 2. If the Network Name SSID you chose requires WEP security settings, you must enter the correct settings. You can use the Networks tab page to identify whether or not the particular SSID you will use requires WEP settings. After checking the \'93
Enable Encryption\'94 checkbox, enter the passphrase, key size, or manual key settings that the wireless network requires. A red lock symbol in the status bar indicates WEP is required or the WEP key is incorrect. 
\par 3. Click Apply to activate your settings.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li180\ri0\sb120\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin180\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\fs18\cf1 How to Save Settings in a Profile
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 \f1\fs18\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {1. Enter a name for the profile in the \'93Profiles\'94 name field. 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li432\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin432\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\fs18\cf1 Tip}{\f1\fs18\cf1 
: If you use a notebook PC to connect to a wireless network at work and at home, create profiles called \'93work\'94 and \'93home.\'94 Then, activate whichever one you need for wherever you are located.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 \f1\fs18\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {2. Click Save Profile. 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li180\ri0\sb60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin180\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\fs18\cf1 How to Start a Computer-to-Computer (Ad Hoc) Network
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 \f1\fs18\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {1. Fill in the Network Name (SSID).
\par 2. Select the Computer-to-Computer (Ad Hoc) network type.
\par 3. Click Initiate Ad Hoc. The Ad Hoc Setting dialog box will appear.
\par 4. If you use a wireless that supports multiple wireless standards (802.11b, or 802.11g), select the standard and channel you want to use.
\par 5. Click Ok.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li180\ri0\sb60\sl200\slmult0\keepn\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin180\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\fs18\cf1 How to Configure Ad Hoc Settings
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 \f1\fs18\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {1. In the Start Ad Hoc field, choose the wireless standard (802.11b, or 80
2.11g) for your Ad Hoc computer-to-computer network.
\par 2. In the Channel field, Automatic should work. If you notice interference problems with another nearby wireless device, select a channel that is not being used by any other wireless networks near your w
ireless adapter. Use the Networks tab page to identify the channels in use in your area.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs18\cf1 3. Click Ok.
\par }\pard \ql \li180\ri0\sb60\sl200\slmult0\keepn\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin180\itap0 {\b\f1\fs18\cf1 Advanced Settings
\par }\pard \ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin180\itap0 {\f1\fs18\cf1 Generally, these settings should not require adjustment. Except for the power saving setting, changing any of the settings incorrectly o
n this page could cause your wireless connection to fail.
\par }\pard \ql \li180\ri0\sa60\sl200\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx360\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin180\itap0 {\b\f1\fs18\cf1 Power Saving}{\f1\fs18\cf1 : Select On if you are using a notebook computer running on battery power.
\par }{\b\f1\fs18\cf1 Preamble}{\f1\fs18\cf1 : A long transmit preamble may provide a more reliable connection or slightly longer range.  A short transmit preamble might give slightly better performance. 
\par }{\b\f1\fs18\cf1 Transmit Power}{\f1\fs18\cf1 : Lowering the output power level lets you reduce the chance of interference with other nearby access points but reduces the range of your adapter.
\par }{\b\f1\fs18\cf1 Wireless Mode}{\f1\fs18\cf1 : Select the wireless protocols you will use. Depending on your wireless adapter, you can choose some or all of the available 802.11 wireless protocols.
\par }{\b\f1\fs18\cf1 Fragmentation Threshold}{\f1\fs18\cf1 : This is the maximum packet size used for fragmentation. Packets larger than the size programmed in thi
s field will be fragmented. The Fragment Threshold value must be larger than the RTS Threshold value.
\par }{\b\f1\fs18\cf1 RTS/CTS Threshold}{\f1\fs18\cf1 : The packet size that is used to determine whether it should use the CSMA/CD (Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection) me
chanism or the CSMA/CA (Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance) mechanism for packet transmission. CSMA/CD is slightly more efficient.
\par }}